# ### EDA Summary
# Missing Values:
# During the exploratory data analysis phase, I noted several characteristics of the dataset that will influence my data processing decisions. First and foremost, a large number of features contain missing values. Some features, such as host_response_rate, host_acceptance_rate, host_about, and neighborhood_overview were missing data for 9000+ listings. Other features such as bedrooms and beds contained missing values to a lesser extent. For each numerical feature with missing values, I computed the correlation between it and the label to be predicted. I noticed that the features with a larger (9000+) number of missing values were among the least correlated with the label. For that reason, I decided that they should be omitted from the feature set I train the model on. By contrast, features with less missing values like bedroom and beds were highly correlated with the label. Since they were seemingly some of the best predictors, I knew they needed to remain within the used feature set but the tuples with missing values needed to be removed from the data set during preprocessing.
# 
# Feature/Label Relationships + Patterns:
# Since the target/label (price_category) was created from the listing price, I expected the original price variable to have the strongest correlation. Aside from price, features such as accommodates, bedrooms, beds, and bathrooms showed the strongest correlation with the label, suggesting that larger properties are more likely to fall into the higher price category. Most other numerical variables had relatively weak correlations, indicating that they can potentially be removed from the feature set to reduce model complexity and overfitting. It seems that no one feature can strongly predict the label on its own.
# 
# Addressing Class Imbalance:
# The dataset does depict class imbalance with there being a larger number of low-priced listings than high-priced listings. Since this imbalance could push the model to default to the majority class, I think it is best if I stratify the train-test split batch on class_weight="balanced" parameter during model training. This way I can reduce potential model bias.
# 
# Non Numerical Values:
# The dataset also contains several categorical variables such as room type, neighborhood, property type (to name a few) that must be encoded numerically before they can be used by the models. 

# ### Ethical Considerations:
#The dataset consists entirely of Airbnb listings in New York at a single time period. This means that the findings from it can only be generalized to New York, not any other state in the US or country in the world, particularly since property prices in New York are relatively higher than listings in other places of residence. Furthermore, these listings were extracted from one platform. Hosts whose airbnbs are not featured on this platform will not be represented either and the data may not represent the greater housing market within New York even. The data may not even reflect the landscape of today's housing/airbnb market depending on when it was last updated, particularly within changes in governor/mayoral terms. Some features such as neighborhood or neighborhood group could serve as proxies for income level, socioeconomic status or race. This means that these protected characteristics and inexisting equalities could be perpetuated by the model if it depends more on these feature variables. 
# By reinforcing existing systemic pricing patterns, the model could unconsciously put hosts in particular neighborhoods at a disadvantage by overestimating or underestimating the value of their listings. 
# Hosts can be harmed by a model that makes incorrect predictions on this data because they are using said predictions as a factor in determining the official price of their listings. If the model misclassifies a listing by either underestimating it or overestimating it, they could lose a greater amount of money over time or have fewer bookings. Both may result in a host being either taken advantage of or forced to shut down because they cannot justify the operating and flat costs to keep their business running. The impact the model could have on each host would likely vary based on how dependent the host is on the model's predictions. For hosts who have spent less time pricing, they may rely on the model's recommendations more than hosts who are more experienced. 

