# ### Data Preparation Summary:
# Cell 1: As mentioned in Summary for Part 3, four columns were removed because they contained a very large number of missing values (9,800 to 11,800 values). Each of these features were incredibly weakly correlated to the label. Therefore, there was minimal benefit in keeping them as having features with a large proportion of missing values would reduce model reliability and introduce additional noise into the dataset.
# 
# Cell 2: Even after removing columns with 9000+ missing values the dataset still had columns with missing values. However, these columns were among those that were greatly correlated with the label. This means they should be included in the data set but the tuples with missing values needed to be imputed. I used median imputation for numerical variables because medians are more robust against outliers. For categorical variables I filled the missing value with "Unknown" as to not introduce new and incorrect categories.
# 
# Cell 3: I removed any price adjacent column because the label was directly created from those price columns. Keeping 'price' as a feature would lead the model to cheat and use its value as the greatest prediction determiner. This would grant it unrealistic performance since generally it will not know the price at all.
# 
# Cell 4: First I removed categorical features that are entirely unique because training a model on a one-hot-encoded feature that has entirely unique values is computationally expensive and will cause my kernel to regularly die. To use logistic regression all inputs must be numerical. Therefore I needed to convert all categorical values in indicator variables using one-hot encoding. 
# 
# Cell 5: I wanted to verify that truly after all the preprocessing steps I took there were indeed no remaining missing values and every feature was numeric. 

