# When comparing the metrics of the two models, both the average accuracy and F1 score was comparatively weaker for the Logistic Regression model. The average accuracy of the Logistic Regression Model was about 74.17% while the Neural Network accuracy was about 82.73%, meaning there was about an 11.5% increase in model accuracy. The F1 scores of both models was 0.85 and 0.89 with the Neural Network model having a slightly better F1 score. 
# While the neural networks were more complex to design, tune, and interpret than logistic regression, I do believe the added complexity was worth it for this context. As mentioned in question 1, the neural network outperformed logistic regression both when it comes to accuracy and F1 scores. This means that the neural network was able to learn the complex non-linear patterns from the data that the Logistic Regression model could not. Since the metric improvement between the models was substantial and allowed for better overall prediction performance, building the neural network is justified.
# If I were presenting these results to the business, I would recommend deploying the neural network. While it does require more time to train and is less interpretable than a logistic regression model, it has better predictive performance than a Logistic regression model with an accuracy of 82.73% compared to 74.17%  and an F1 score of 0.889 compared to 0.851. These improvements are significant and indicate that a neural network is better able to balance precision and recall ability when it comes to correctly classifying listings as high or low priced. Since the purpose of the model is to correctly classify listings as low price or high price before they are listed and the neural network reduces errors in classification, it is better to use a neural network than logistic regression. Training a neural network model though costly is not a recurring cost within one state unless the economic landscape drastically changes and the data the model is trained on changes as well. If the client must  explain to each individual AirBNB host how the model reaches a prediction to help the hosts identify what factors they should improve to increase their listing price classification, then I would recommend a logistic regression model. Logistic regression models are more interpretable. Overall, the neural network is best at balancing optimal  predictive performance and generalization ability for classifying listings.
# If I had more time, I would focus on improving the overall quality of the neural network model. My first step would be to test an increasing number of hyperparameter combinations rather than settling on the two layer 64 and 32 neurons with a learning rate of 0.001. I would use grid search to test different depths, neuron counts, learning rates, and optimization functions. Also, since the training curves started to overfit after 15-20 epochs, I would implement early stopping so that weights are not updated once a certain difference threshold is met. This would reduce overfitting chances and improve the model's ability to generalize. I would also spend more time on feature engineering by pruning/deciding my feature set more carefully and evaluating whether it truly provides meaningful information that contributes to label prediction. While I tried to remove noisy features from my initial feature set, I was too afraid of removing the wrong features which means my feature set is not optimized. Since many of these features are one-hot-encoded, the time spent training the model is increased for features that are not conducive to a prediction. I would like to further analyze feature importance and look at scaling skewed numerical features. 
